---
prompt:
  schemaStrictness: retry
output:
  schema:
    type: object
    additionalProperties: false
    required: [groups, ignore]
    properties:
      groups:
        type: array
        description: "logical groups of related files; each becomes one commit"
        minItems: 1
{{#if maxCommits}}
        maxItems: {{maxCommits}}
{{/if}}
        items:
          type: object
          additionalProperties: false
          required: [label, files]
          properties:
            label:
              type: string
              maxLength: 40
              description: "conventional-commit-style label describing this group's intent, e.g. 'feat: agent check loop'"
            files:
              type: array
              description: "repo-relative paths belonging to this commit, exactly as listed in the summary"
              items:
                type: string
      ignore:
        type: array
        description: "paths to exclude from real commits (lock files, build artifacts, generated bundles, vendored output); committed separately as a chore commit"
        items:
          type: string
---
You are organizing a set of uncommitted changes into clean, logical git commits.

The changed files are listed below with their code scope (derived from the repository map), git status, and +added/-deleted line counts.

{{table}}

Produce the final commit grouping. Rules:
- Each group MUST represent a single high-level change — one feature, one fix, or one refactor. NEVER put unrelated changes in the same group.
- Group by logical change, one feature/fix/refactor per commit. The Scope column is a hint, not a hard boundary — split or merge across scopes as the change intent dictates.
{{#if maxCommits}}
- Produce at most {{maxCommits}} commits (the chore commit for lock/generated files does NOT count toward this limit). If there are more logical groups, merge the smallest related ones until you are within the limit.
{{/if}}
- Keep a test file in the same group as the feature it tests.
- Every changed file MUST appear in exactly one group's "files" or in "ignore".
- Put lock files, build artifacts, generated bundles, and vendored output in "ignore" — they are committed separately as a chore commit and never analyzed.
- Use repo-relative paths exactly as shown above (for renames, use the new path).
- Give each group a short conventional-commit-style label describing its intent.
